            "body": "It can launch the container somehow but the internal logic seems not correct. I think libs under `/opt/venv/lib` are not the ones we intended to use, at least for current low level stack. ",
            "createdAt": "2026-07-15T04:52:19Z",
            "databaseId": 4976981175,
            "updatedAt": "2026-07-15T04:52:19Z",
            "url": "https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/48634#issuecomment-4976981175"
          },
          {
            "author": {
              "__typename": "User",
              "login": "marcorigodanzo"
            },
            "body": "@yma11 Your instinct is right that there are two candidate sets of libs here — but I think the data shows the `/opt/venv/lib` ones are exactly the intended ones. Here is what I measured inside a running stock `0.25.1` XPU container (2× Arc Pro B70, serving in production for 8 days with `/opt/venv/lib` prepended to `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`).\n\n### The image ships two *different builds* of the same SYCL/UR sonames\n\nBoth `/opt/venv/lib` and `/opt/intel/oneapi/compiler/2025.3/lib` provide `libsycl.so.8`, `libur_loader.so.0.12.0` and `libur_adapter_level_zero.so.0.12.0` — same sonames, same version numbers, **different files**:\n\n| library | `/opt/venv/lib` | `/opt/intel/oneapi/compiler/2025.3/lib` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `libsycl.so.8.0.0` | `0336997fdfed9b2e…`  11812104 B | `18fa367fb7be21f0…`  11812104 B |\n| `libur_loader.so.0.12.0` | `68e273791752638d…`  24405248 B | `32d668526c7e76ed…`  24405**632** B |\n| `libur_adapter_level_zero.so.0.12.0` | `ceecac1cb3124f15…`  4082872 B | `790057ec30cbac2f…`  4082**936** B |\n\nThe cause is a patch-level mismatch: the pip wheels are `intel-sycl-rt==2025.3.2` and `intel-cmplr-lib-ur==2025.3.2`, while the oneAPI install in the image is `2025.3`. Same soname, different build.\n\n### What a working engine actually loads\n\nFrom `/proc/<engine-pid>/maps` of the running server:\n\n```\n/opt/venv/lib/libsycl.so.8\n/opt/venv/lib/libur_loader.so.0\n/opt/venv/lib/libur_adapter_level_zero.so.0\n/opt/venv/lib/libur_adapter_level_zero_v2.so.0\n/opt/venv/lib/libur_adapter_opencl.so.0\n/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libze_loader.so.1.28.2\n/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libze_intel_gpu.so.1.15.38308\n```\n\nSo the working configuration is **pip-wheel SYCL/UR + system Level Zero**, and `torch 2.12.0+xpu` is the wheel built against the `2025.3.2` runtime.\n\n### Why the fix is needed at all\n\n`/opt/intel/oneapi/compiler/2025.3/lib` is *already* on the baked `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` (line 139). So without this PR the loader resolves the **other** build first, and torch reports 0 XPU devices — which is the reported symptom. The libs under `/opt/venv/lib` aren't stray leftovers; they're the ones the torch XPU wheel depends on, and the ones a working container demonstrably resolves.\n\n### Suggestion\n\nThe PR fixes the symptom correctly and matches what works in the field. If you want to address the root cause rather than the ordering, the options would be to stop shipping two overlapping SYCL runtimes in the same image, or to pin the oneAPI install and the pip wheels to the same patch level — because as it stands, which runtime wins is decided purely by `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` ordering, which is fragile regardless of what this PR sets it to.\n\nHappy to run further checks inside the container if any would help.\n",
